2025-05-01 03:18:02 - Thread-1321 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Relevant columns and their potential usage for building monthly closing balances  
• customer_transactions."customer_id": customer identifier – used to partition the running balance  
• customer_transactions."txn_date": transaction timestamp – used to sort chronologically and to derive “year‑month” buckets  
• customer_transactions."txn_type": ‘deposit’ or ‘withdrawal’ – will decide whether "txn_amount" is added or subtracted from the balance  
• customer_transactions."txn_amount": signed transaction value – aggregated to obtain the monthly net change and cumulative balance  
• customer_nodes."region_id": optional ‑ if regional reporting is required  
• customer_regions."region_name": human‑readable region label (join via customer_nodes.region_id = customer_regions.region_id)  

Typical filter / join conditions you might apply later  
• WHERE customer_transactions."txn_type" = 'deposit'            -- only inflows  
• WHERE customer_transactions."txn_type" = 'withdrawal'         -- only outflows  
• WHERE customer_transactions."txn_date" BETWEEN '2020-01-01' AND '2020-12-31'  
• JOIN customer_nodes            ON customer_transactions."customer_id" = customer_nodes."customer_id"  
• JOIN customer_regions          ON customer_nodes."region_id"   = customer_regions."region_id"  

Below are ten exploratory SQL snippets (LIMIT 20 rows each) that gradually move from simple inspection to more advanced checks.  
Each query is preceded by a short description.

```sql
-- 1) Description: List the distinct transaction types that exist in the ledger
SELECT DISTINCT "txn_type"
FROM "customer_transactions"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Description: Inspect a sample of raw transaction dates to understand their format
SELECT DISTINCT "txn_date"
FROM "customer_transactions"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Description: Get a quick look at customer IDs present in the transactions table
SELECT DISTINCT "customer_id"
FROM "customer_transactions"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Description: Extract distinct calendar year‑month buckets in YYYY‑MM form
SELECT DISTINCT substr("txn_date", 1, 7) AS "year_month"
FROM "customer_transactions"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Description: Sample deposits larger than 1,000 to see magnitude of inflows
SELECT DISTINCT "customer_id", "txn_date", "txn_amount"
FROM "customer_transactions"
WHERE "txn_type" = 'deposit'
  AND "txn_amount" > 1000
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Description: Sample withdrawals (outflows) and their absolute values
SELECT DISTINCT "customer_id", "txn_date", "txn_amount"
FROM "customer_transactions"
WHERE "txn_type" = 'withdrawal'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Description: For each customer, view their last recorded transaction date (simple max aggregation)
SELECT "customer_id",
       MAX("txn_date") AS "last_txn_date"
FROM "customer_transactions"
GROUP BY "customer_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Description: Combine transactions with node data to see which regions have activity
SELECT DISTINCT ct."customer_id",
                cr."region_name"
FROM   "customer_transactions" AS ct
JOIN   "customer_nodes"        AS cn ON ct."customer_id" = cn."customer_id"
JOIN   "customer_regions"      AS cr ON cn."region_id"   = cr."region_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Description: Show monthly net change per customer (deposits minus withdrawals)
SELECT ct."customer_id",
       substr(ct."txn_date", 1, 7) AS "year_month",
       SUM(CASE WHEN ct."txn_type" = 'deposit'     THEN  ct."txn_amount"
                WHEN ct."txn_type" = 'withdrawal' THEN -ct."txn_amount"
           END) AS "monthly_net_change"
FROM   "customer_transactions" AS ct
GROUP  BY ct."customer_id", substr(ct."txn_date", 1, 7)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10) Description: Compute a cumulative running balance per customer ordered by month
-- (window function example – helps validate the final closing‑balance logic)
SELECT "customer_id",
       "year_month",
       "monthly_net_change",
       SUM("monthly_net_change") OVER (
            PARTITION BY "customer_id"
            ORDER BY "year_month"
            ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW
       ) AS "cumulative_balance"
FROM (
    SELECT ct."customer_id",
           substr(ct."txn_date", 1, 7) AS "year_month",
           SUM(CASE WHEN ct."txn_type" = 'deposit'     THEN  ct."txn_amount"
                    WHEN ct."txn_type" = 'withdrawal' THEN -ct."txn_amount"
               END) AS "monthly_net_change"
    FROM   "customer_transactions" AS ct
    GROUP  BY ct."customer_id", substr(ct."txn_date", 1, 7)
) AS m
LIMIT 20;
```

These exploration steps map out the critical columns, reveal the data patterns you’ll need, and build up to a window‑function approach capable of outputting, for every customer and every month (even months with no activity once you introduce a calendar spine), the monthly change plus the cumulative closing balance.
